- Frequency of reading books: Reading "On everyday basis" or "A few times a week" suggests frequent reading, possibly academic.

---

Step 2. Based on the above analysis and example, infer 10 different conditions per answer class.

10 different conditions for class "0" (Does NOT read academic books most of the time):
- Reason for reading books is in [Entertainment Purpose, To increase my English vocabulary knowledge]
- Amount of time spent reading books per day < 1
- Frequency of reading books is in [Rarely, Once a week, Less than once a week]
- Number of e-books you read last two years is in [nan, 1-5]
- Number of printed books you read last two years is in [nan, 1-5]
- Favorite books for reading to spend leisure time is in [Fiction, Politics, Others]
- What is the change that you felt after reading books is not "Increase Academic Performance" (or is in [None, Increase... (non-academic changes)])
- Department is in [BBA, English, JMC, Textile, NFE]
- Who influenced you to read books is in [Parents, Friends] (less self-driven)
- The format that you used for reading books is in [E-books, Borrow] only (less varied reading habits)

10 different conditions for class "1" (Reads academic books most of the time):
- Reason for reading books is Academic Purpose
- Amount of time spent reading books per day >= 1
- Frequency of reading books is in [On everyday basis, A few times a week]
- Number of e-books you read last two years is in [6-10, 11-15, 16-20, More than 20]
- Number of printed books you read last two years is in [6-10, 11-15, 16-20, More than 20]
- What is the change that you felt after reading books is Increase Academic Performance
- Department is in [CSE, SWE, Pharmacy, EEE, Civil, CIS, MCT]
- Who influenced you to read books is Self-Decision
- The format that you used for reading books is Both
- Favorite books for reading to spend leisure time is in [Non-Fiction, Biography]

---

This completes the condition extraction for both answer classes.

---DIVIDER---

Step 1. The relationship between each feature and the task description:

- Gender: Gender itself does not strongly influence whether a student reads academic books most of the time.
- Age: Older undergraduates might have more academic responsibilities, possibly reading academic books more often.
- Department: Students from technical or science departments (CSE, SWE, Pharmacy, EEE, Civil, Textile, MCT, CIS) are more likely to read academic books most of the time compared to departments like English or BBA where leisure reading might be more common.
- Amount of time spent reading books per day: More time spent reading books per day likely correlates with reading academic books most of the time.
- Reason for reading books: If the reason is "Academic Purpose," the student likely reads academic books most of the time.
- How to manage books for reading: Method alone may not strongly influence academic reading, but E-books or Borrow may indicate active academic reading habits.
- Number of e-books read last two years: Higher numbers suggest more academic reading, especially if combined with other academic indicators.
- Number of printed books read last two years: Similarly, a higher number indicates more academic reading.
- Favorite books for leisure reading: If leisure reading is mostly Fiction or Politics, less likely to be academic reading most of the time; if Non-Fiction or Biography, possibly more academic or serious reading.
- What is the change felt after reading books: If the change is "Increase Academic Performance," it strongly indicates academic reading.
- Who influenced you to read books: Influence by parents or teachers might correlate with academic reading habits.
- Which language books do you like to read: Preference for English may correlate with academic reading.
- Where do you read printed books: Reading at home or library may be more academic.
- Do you read Newspaper: Reading newspapers might indicate general reading habits but not necessarily academic reading.
- The format used for reading books: Using both E-books and printed books may indicate serious reading habits.
- Frequency of reading books: Reading on an everyday basis strongly correlates with reading academic books most of the time.

---

